    "figures": [
        "1. Figure 1: Comparison of visual token pruning approaches. (Left) D2Pruner requires offline calibration on external data to compute bias priors before deployment. (Right) Our method uses deep-layer (L12) attention directly during inference, eliminating offline calibration while achieving superior performance through semantically richer attention patterns.",
        "2. Figure 2: Sensitivity of ratio-based debiasing to shallow layer choice (Ks). Performance varies by 6.47 points across Ks values. Raw attention (dashed line) consistently outperforms all ratio variants.",
        "3. Figure 3: Spatial analysis of token retention. Our method achieves 43% higher token-bbox IoU (0.212) than FastV (0.148), indicating better concentration of retained tokens around referred objects."
    ],
    "results_section": [
        "1. The improvement is consistent across all 8 benchmark splits, with gains ranging from +9.55 points (RefCOCO+ testB) to +13.64 points (RefCOCO+ val).",
        "2. Compared to the no-pruning upper bound (72.06%), our method retains 92.0% of the original performance while reducing visual tokens by 90%.",
        "3. In contrast, D2Pruner retains only 76.4% of no-pruning performance, and FastV retains just 47.0%.",
        "4. Figure 2 shows that ratio-based debiasing is sensitive to the choice of shallow layer Ks, with performance varying by 6.47 points across configurations.",
        "5. Our method achieves 43% higher token-bbox IoU (0.212) compared to FastV (0.148) and 20% higher than D2Pruner (0.177)."
    ]
